═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
FULL HAMILTONIAN — WHAT IT CHANGES
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

DEFAULT (no flag):      restricts to the Sz = 0 sector.
                        Dim = C(6,3)·C(6,3) = 400 for L=6, Ne=6.
                        Contains the ground state and all singlet states.

--full-hamiltonian:     keeps all Sz sectors (Sz = −3 … +3).
                        Dim = C(12,6) = 924 for L=6, Ne=6.
                        Adds triplets (Sz=±1) and higher multiplets — matters
                        at higher temperature where they gain Boltzmann weight.

The nBRDM / GCG machinery is identical in both modes; only the eigenstate set
fed into the thermal sum changes. Output filenames carry an “…_full…” tag so
the two modes never overwrite each other.


═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
SYSTEM SIZE & MEMORY LIMITS  (read before increasing L)
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

Hilbert-space dimension:
     Sz = 0 sector   dim = C(L, Ne/2)^2
     Full (all Sz)   dim = C(2L, Ne)

All four tasks are THERMAL — they need the FULL spectrum (dense eigh in
compute_all_eigenstates) AND precompute an RDM for every eigenstate
(the “RDM bank”). Both scale with dim, so RAM explodes fast.

  L   Ne | Sz=0 dim     | dense eigh (~2·dim²·16B) | RDM bank[n=L] (dim·4^L·16B)
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   6    6 |          400 | 4.9 MB                   | 25 MB      ← current, fine
   8    8 |        4,900 | 733 MB                   | 4.8 GB
  10   10 |       63,504 | 120 GB                   | ~1 TB      ← already impractical
  12   10 |      627,264 | 11.5 TB                  | 153 TB
  20   10 |  240,374,016 | 1.6 EB                   | 3.6 ZB     ← physically impossible

PRACTICAL CEILING for the thermal tasks (Tasks 1–4): L ≈ 8, maybe 10 on a
big-RAM node. L ≥ 12 is not feasible with full diagonalization + RDM bank.

Why the RDM bank is the killer: it stores ONE reduced density matrix PER
eigenstate so it can reuse them across all temperatures. One RDM is small
(16 MB at L=10); holding all `dim` of them (63,504 at L=10) is what reaches
~1 TB. The cost is the “× every eigenstate” factor, not any single RDM.

To go larger you must leave full diagonalization behind:
     • ground state only  → sparse eigsh, ONE RDM  (≈16 MB at L=10, ~180 GB
                            sparse H at L=20 — HPC node, not a laptop),
     • or DMRG / MPS for the many-body state at larger L.
The thermal sum over the whole spectrum is the hard wall.


